    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis, continuation phase (A15.0)</content>. Intensive phase completed on time with a full clinical response. Liver tests normal (ALT 29, AST 27, bilirubin 0.6). Hemoglobin 13.0, up from 12.1. Dr. Kowalczyk-Grant reviewed the results by message and agrees with continuing. [S]<list><item>Rifapentine 1200 mg, moxifloxacin 400 mg, isoniazid 300 mg, and pyridoxine daily by video for 63 doses.</item><item>Two-month sputum smear and culture today, from the morning sample.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Moderate protein-calorie malnutrition (E44.0)</content>. Improving: BMI 18.0. [S]<list><item>Continue the supplements until his weight reaches 55 kg.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Symptom review: cough, sputum, hemoptysis, fever, night sweats</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Morning phlegm only</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, with the pyrazinamide dose checked against the weight band</td><td>Monthly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>52.0 kg; pyrazinamide finished within its band</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects: hepatitis symptoms, tendon pain, numbness or tingling, rash, nausea, orange body fluids</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (ALT, AST, bilirubin)</td><td>Baseline; repeat for symptoms or risk factors (here at two to three weeks, for low weight and albumin)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>ALT 29, AST 27 U/L, bilirubin 0.6 mg/dL, all normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin</td><td>When low at baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>13.0 g/dL, up from 12.1; anemia of inflammation resolving</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Phenotypic susceptibility (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, moxifloxacin)</td><td>At baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Susceptible to rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and moxifloxacin</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sputum acid-fast smear and culture</td><td>Baseline, then monthly until two consecutive cultures are negative</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Third-week cultures: no growth at six weeks (first negative culture)</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Two-month sputum culture</td><td>At the end of the intensive phase; a positive result needs evaluation and repeat susceptibility testing</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Smear negative today; culture sent</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Chest X-ray</td><td>Baseline; at two months only if baseline cultures were negative; end of treatment optional</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not needed at two months, because the baseline cultures were positive</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Doses observed and the completion clock (56 doses within 70 days, 63 within 84 days, 119 within five months)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>56 doses in 56 days, within the 70-day limit</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Contact investigation (students, friends, close contacts)</td><td>Led by the health department from diagnosis</td><td>Referred</td><td>Continuing with the health department; no results in his record</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At diagnosis, at two months, and at the end of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Nutrition: dietitian, intake, supplement drinks</td><td>Every visit until weight is restored</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Supplements continue; eating well</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Infectious diseases review</td><td>At diagnosis, at two months, and at the end of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Results reviewed by message today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Complete all 119 doses within five months by video-observed therapy. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>Sputum culture negative at two months. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>Return to teaching once the health department confirms he is no longer infectious. Outcome by 1838-04-07: Released from isolation after three negative smears and more than two weeks of treatment; teaching again from the third week [S]</item><item>Weight back to at least 55 kg by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 5 by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1838-07-21 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); Interim Guidance: 4-Month Rifapentine-Moxifloxacin Regimen for the Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Pulmonary Tuberculosis - United States, 2022 (2022); An official ATS statement: hepatotoxicity of antituberculosis therapy (2006); Guidelines for the investigation of contacts of persons with infectious tuberculosis: recommendations from the National Tuberculosis Controllers Association and CDC (2005); Consensus statement: Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics and American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition: characteristics recommended for the identification and documentation of adult malnutrition (undernutrition) (2012); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: End-of-treatment visit after the 119th dose. Call the same day for hepatitis symptoms, tendon pain, or a returning cough. Next scheduled visit 1838-07-21: end-of-treatment review after 119 doses. [S]</paragraph></text>

